 ### [Ray-box intersection](776-ray-box-intersection.md)
-Computes the parametric entry ($t_{near}$) and exit ($t_{far}$) distances of a ray intersecting an Axis-Aligned Bounding Box (AABB). It solves the problem of determining the valid sampling interval along a ray for volume rendering, ensuring that density or color sampling occurs only within the volume's physical bounds. Unlike [Ray-box intersection via OpenGL Geometry Shaders](1768-ray-box-intersection-via-opengl-geometry-shaders.md) which rasterizes the box to derive intersection data, this method performs an analytical calculation. It sets itself apart from [Ray-AABB Intersection (Slab/Min-Max)](6548-ray-aabb-intersection-slab-min-max.md) by explicitly providing the scalar interval $[t_{min}, t_{max}]$ required for subsequent volume sampling or traversal, rather than just a boolean hit test [G].
+The Ray-box intersection method (commonly known as the Slab Test) computes the parametric entry ($t_{near}$) and exit ($t_{far}$) distances where a ray intersects an Axis-Aligned Bounding Box (AABB). It serves as the fundamental primitive for [Ray-Bounding Box Intersection and Sampling Preparation](2364-ray-bounding-box-intersection-and-sampling-preparation.md) in volume rendering pipelines.
 
 ### [Ray-Volume Intersection (Face Distance Method)](2381-ray-volume-intersection-face-distance-method.md)
-A two-step procedure to determine if a viewing ray intersects the data volume and to find the entry point for volume traversal. It calculates distances from the ray origin to each of the six faces of the volume geometry, divides by the corresponding ray direction component, and selects the smallest valid distance as the nearest impact point.
+This method determines if a viewing ray intersects the bounding volume of a dataset and calculates the entry point for subsequent volume traversal. It serves as the initial geometric culling step in ray casting, ensuring that rays missing the data volume are discarded before expensive sampling occurs. Unlike [Ray-box intersection](776-ray-box-intersection.md), which typically uses slab-based interval intersection for axis-aligned bounding boxes, this method explicitly computes distances to the six faces of the data volume to determine a planar impact coordinate. It is distinct from [Homogeneous Volume Intersection](3260-homogeneous-volume-intersection.md) by not assuming uniform density for analytical free-path calculation, and from [Near/Far Distance Precomputation Pass](3576-near-far-distance-precomputation-pass.md) by using analytical geometry rather than rasterization to find bounds[G].
 
 ### [Ray Filtering](2681-ray-filtering.md)
-Reduces computational load by discarding rays that do not intersect with the scene's geometry or alpha mask before expensive volume rendering steps. It distinguishes itself from sibling methods by operating as a pre-processing culling stage on a batch of rays, often integrating alpha-mask density checks alongside axis-aligned bounding box (AABB) intersection tests.
+Ray filtering reduces computational load by discarding rays that do not intersect with the scene's geometry or alpha mask before expensive volume rendering steps. It distinguishes itself from sibling methods by operating as a pre-processing culling stage on a batch of rays, often integrating alpha-mask density checks alongside axis-aligned bounding box (AABB) intersection tests.
 
 ### [Procedural Ray/Box Intersection](8356-procedural-ray-box-intersection.md)
-Determines ray entry and exit within a volume's bounding box entirely within the fragment shader, without pre-rendering geometry. It computes the ray's intersection with the bounding box directly in the shader, allowing the volume to be rendered as a simple screen-aligned quad or similar primitive. Unlike rasterization-based setup methods that use geometry to pass ray data, this approach is self-contained but places the full computational load of intersection and marching on the fragment shader.
